rent devices i've  got a few screenshots here  where i'm comparing and contrasting  but if you create content in mathematica  online  which we just saw cliff do where he  created a series of calculations  if you save that content and then log in  in your local installation of  mathematica  you can pull up that notebook and  interact with that content  and it's all very interchangeable which  is nice  but what's even more useful is you can  log into mathematica online  from any web-enabled device so even  something like a chromebook  where that device is less powerful than  a full windows or a full mac  machine because the calculations are  happening  on our server and because there's no  plugin that's needed  you can log in from as i said anything  that runs a browser  and you can pull up your notebook again  or start a new notebook or append to  your work  but the the variety of  available devices is much much greater  with mathematica online  we also have an app so if you're using  something like an ipad  we've got a wolfram cloud app it's  available on both  ios and android but the idea is that  you pull up the app and that helps um  with a you know it helps provide a very  convenient keyboard  it helps layout the notebook very  conveniently but  in general here the notebook looks  exactly the same which is  precisely what we want and on an ipad  you know the app is still tunneling to  our servers to do the calculation  and but the look of it the look of the  notebook and the authoring environment  is exactly the same same is true with an  iphone  so even on that smaller screen the text  the calculations the titles are all very  usable  if you've got an example with a slider  you can even move the slider with your  finger  similar to how how you would do that  with a mouse on a full installation or  with a mouse in mathematic online  so the the availability across  devices is very very good with  mathematic online  because everything looks the same and a  notebook is a notebook  on any of those devices  next thing i want to talk about is  publishing a notebook so this gets more  into sharing a finished  project i'm going to quickly show the  workflow of this where i'm  back in mathematica online here this is  the same notebook  after you've got a project that's  completed you can  click the publish button up here you can  give your project a different name  or you can password protect it but when  you click this publish button  mathematica online generates a different  url  a public-facing url which you can either  copy  or frame into an existing website so you  can embed the content  but when you view the page this second  url  again anyone can read this text anyone  can look at the calculations  and anyone can move the slider to do  some mouse driven calculations as well  no installation is needed they just need  to go to this url  so let me drill in a little bit on how  that works um  you can publish a notebook either from a  local installation of mathematica  or mathematica online then  as i said the recipient anyone can look  at this but  the recipient can also do some  mouse-driven calculations  when they do those mouse-driven  calculations they use the author's cloud  credits  meaning the author has a pool of time on  our servers that refreshes monthly  it's a it's a pretty um a pretty healthy  pool  of of cloud credits per month and it  refreshes  every month but that's how the  recipients when they move those sliders  that's how it works where the cloud  credits of the author  powers those calculations  and then in general a published notebook  can be included  in really any web page so if you have a  personal  project where you want more calculations  it's a good use for that  if you have an academic publication you  can include a published notebook  and the link to that published notebook  so people reading your article  they can go off and look at more  graphics or more supporting calculations  very easily or this is really nice for  course materials so  i'll talk about this in a minute but  this is a nice way to post course  materials where  everybody can look at it as a preview  and then start to use it and interact  with it  so that leads me to adapting a published  notebook where  if you want to edit a published notebook  in mathematica or mathematica online  there's a really easy way to do that as  well  so i'm going to click back here to this  published notebook  the two ways to do this are the make  your own copy  and the download button so as the  recipient of this notebook as the reader  if i click make your own copy this  prompts me to log into my copy of  mathematica online  and it copies the um it copies the  notebook into my account  and then this is all now a live and  editable notebook after it copies  so if i go in here and say two times x  and reevaluate this  you know this is all now a live notebook  again  so let me drill in and talk about how  this works as well so  if you're if the recipient of your  notebook  is a mathematica online user they can  click make your own copy  and again then they've got a copy where  they can adapt your  notebook for their purposes or they can  append to it or they can take notes  in it download is the same idea but  it downloads a full notebook and then  they can just edit that in a full  mathematica  a local mathematica installation  so colleagues friends or students they  all independently alter calculations so  as i mentioned  when they click make your own copy that  does not affect the  author's copy of the notebook it auto  copies into the recipient's  cloud storage basically  so that's really useful for course  materials and course assignments so  if you want to publish an assignment or  materials  and you know though that the student is  going to take that  and make their own notes or they're  going to read your instructions and then  author their own content  that's a really good way to set this up  and post the 